DATA=[{'filename': 'como-decorar-dormitorio-pequeno.html', 'title': 'How to Decorate a Small Bedroom to Make It Feel More Spacious', 'category': 'HOME DECOR', 'meta': 'Learn how to decorate a small bedroom with a better layout, proportional furniture, smart storage, lighting, color, mirrors, curtains, and carefully scaled textiles.', 'intro': 'A small bedroom can feel comfortable, organized, and visually spacious when layout, furniture, storage, color, and lighting work together.', 'body': "\n<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<ul><li>Introduction</li><li>Plan the Layout Before Decorating</li><li>Choose the Right Bed Position</li><li>Use Furniture With Appropriate Scale</li><li>Take Advantage of Vertical Space</li><li>Use Color to Create Visual Continuity</li><li>Improve Natural and Artificial Light</li><li>Choose Curtains Carefully</li><li>Use Mirrors With Purpose</li><li>Reduce Visual Clutter</li><li>Improve Storage Without Overfilling the Room</li><li>Choose Textiles and Rugs in the Right Proportion</li><li>Create One Clear Focal Point</li><li>Common Mistakes to Avoid</li><li>Frequently Asked Questions</li><li>Conclusion</li></ul>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>A small bedroom can feel comfortable, organized, and visually spacious when the layout, furniture, storage, color, and lighting work together. The goal is not to make the room look empty. It is to use the available area efficiently while preserving comfortable circulation and enough visual breathing room.</p>\n<p>Before buying new furniture or decoration, identify what currently makes the room feel smaller. Oversized furniture, poor lighting, cluttered surfaces, blocked windows, and too many unrelated colors can all contribute. Correcting these issues often creates more improvement than adding decorative objects.</p>\n<h2>Plan the Layout Before Decorating</h2>\n<p>Measure the room, doors, windows, wardrobe openings, and any fixed elements. Draw a simple floor plan or use painter's tape to test furniture positions. Make sure drawers and doors can open completely and that walking routes remain clear.</p>\n<p>Prioritize the pieces that are genuinely necessary. In many bedrooms, the bed and clothing storage are the largest requirements. Additional chairs, benches, or tables should only remain if they serve a real function without restricting movement.</p>\n<p>Leaving some visible floor area can make the room feel calmer. Avoid placing small pieces of furniture in every available corner simply because they fit.</p>\n<h2>Choose the Right Bed Position</h2>\n<p>The bed is usually the largest element and should anchor the layout. When possible, position it where access is comfortable and where it does not block windows, doors, or storage.</p>\n<p>A bed pushed against a wall may save space in some rooms, but it can make access difficult for two people. Evaluate daily use rather than following a fixed design rule.</p>\n<p>Keep the area around the bed visually simple. A clear wall, a restrained headboard, or one piece of artwork can create a stronger sense of order than many small decorations.</p>\n<h2>Use Furniture With Appropriate Scale</h2>\n<p>Small rooms need furniture that matches their dimensions. A very deep dresser or wide bedside table can consume valuable circulation space even when the room technically accommodates it.</p>\n<p>Look for narrow bedside tables, wall-mounted shelves, compact dressers, or multifunctional pieces. Furniture with visible legs can sometimes make more floor area visible, creating a lighter appearance.</p>\n<p>Do not automatically choose miniature furniture. Pieces should still be practical. A bedside table that cannot hold the items you use every night may create more clutter elsewhere.</p>\n<h2>Take Advantage of Vertical Space</h2>\n<p>Walls provide useful storage without consuming additional floor area. Tall shelving, wall-mounted lights, hooks, and shelves above suitable furniture can help organize the room.</p>\n<p>Keep heavy objects secure and avoid placing unstable items above the bed. Follow appropriate installation methods for the wall type and expected load.</p>\n<p>Use upper storage for items that are not needed every day. Frequently used belongings should remain easy to reach so organization does not become inconvenient.</p>\n<h2>Use Color to Create Visual Continuity</h2>\n<p>Light colors can reflect more available light, but a small bedroom does not have to be completely white. Soft neutrals, muted colors, and even deeper shades can work when the palette is coherent.</p>\n<p>Limit the number of competing colors. Repeating one or two accent colors across bedding, artwork, and accessories creates continuity and reduces visual fragmentation.</p>\n<p>Paint the ceiling, trim, and walls thoughtfully. Strong contrast can be attractive, but too many abrupt transitions may make a very small room feel busier.</p>\n<h2>Improve Natural and Artificial Light</h2>\n<p>Keep windows as unobstructed as privacy and comfort allow. Large furniture directly in front of a window can reduce daylight and make the room feel more enclosed.</p>\n<p>At night, use more than one light source when possible. A ceiling fixture combined with bedside or wall lights creates depth and gives different options for reading, dressing, and relaxing.</p>\n<p>Wall-mounted bedside lights can free the top of small nightstands. Choose fixtures and electrical installations appropriate for the room and follow safety requirements.</p>\n<h2>Choose Curtains Carefully</h2>\n<p>Curtains can influence the perceived height and width of a wall. Mounting the rod appropriately above the window and allowing curtains to frame rather than cover most of the glass can preserve daylight.</p>\n<p>Choose the fabric according to privacy, light control, and climate. A bedroom may need a blackout layer, but that can be combined with a lighter curtain for daytime use.</p>\n<p>Measure before purchasing. Curtains that are too short or narrow can make the window look smaller and reduce the polished appearance of the room.</p>\n<h2>Use Mirrors With Purpose</h2>\n<p>A mirror can reflect light and add visual depth, especially when positioned where it reflects an attractive or bright part of the room.</p>\n<p>Do not add many mirrors simply to make the room appear larger. One well-positioned mirror is often more effective and easier to integrate.</p>\n<p>Secure large mirrors correctly, particularly in areas where they could be bumped. Safety and stability are more important than decorative effect.</p>\n<h2>Reduce Visual Clutter</h2>\n<p>Clear dresser tops, bedside tables, and the floor. Visible clutter makes a small room feel even smaller because the eye has fewer calm areas to rest.</p>\n<p>Group small objects in trays or containers rather than scattering them across surfaces. Keep only frequently used or meaningful items visible.</p>\n<p>Cables also contribute to visual clutter. Organize chargers and lamp cords so they do not accumulate around the bed or floor.</p>\n<h2>Improve Storage Without Overfilling the Room</h2>\n<p>Use existing storage efficiently before buying more furniture. Review clothing, remove items no longer used, and organize shelves and drawers by category.</p>\n<p>Under-bed storage can be useful for seasonal bedding, shoes, or less frequently used items. Choose containers that are easy to access and keep stored items protected from dust.</p>\n<p>Storage furniture should not become a reason to keep unnecessary belongings. Reducing the volume of possessions can be more effective than adding another cabinet.</p>\n<h2>Choose Textiles and Rugs in the Right Proportion</h2>\n<p>Bedding occupies a large visual area, so a simple coordinated set can make the room feel more organized. Use texture to create interest without adding too many patterns.</p>\n<p>A rug can soften the room, but size matters. A rug that is too small may visually divide the floor into more sections. Measure the bed and available floor before choosing one.</p>\n<p>Use cushions and throws selectively. Too many decorative layers can overwhelm a compact bedroom and create extra work every day.</p>\n<h2>Create One Clear Focal Point</h2>\n<p>The bed wall is a natural focal point. Artwork, a headboard, a restrained wall color, or coordinated lighting can give the room a clear visual center.</p>\n<p>Keep supporting walls simpler. When every wall contains a strong pattern, gallery, shelf, or large decoration, the room can feel crowded.</p>\n<p>A focal point should help organize the composition rather than become another source of clutter.</p>\n<h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>\n<p>Oversized furniture is one of the most common problems. Measure before buying and account for doors, drawers, and circulation.</p>\n<p>Avoid filling every wall with shelves. Vertical storage is useful, but too many visible objects can create visual weight.</p>\n<p>Do not assume that only white makes a room feel larger. Good lighting, proportion, organization, and a coherent palette are equally important.</p>\n<p>Finally, avoid purchasing storage before decluttering. Otherwise, the room may simply contain more furniture and the same amount of unnecessary material.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>What colors work best in a small bedroom?</h3><p>Light and muted colors are easy to use, but deeper colors can also work. A limited, coherent palette matters more than following one specific color rule.</p>\n<h3>Should the bed be against the wall?</h3><p>Only if that arrangement suits the room and the people using it. Comfortable access and circulation should guide the decision.</p>\n<h3>Do mirrors really make a room feel larger?</h3><p>They can reflect light and add visual depth, especially when positioned thoughtfully, but they cannot compensate for clutter or oversized furniture.</p>\n<h3>How can I add storage without making the room crowded?</h3><p>Use vertical and under-bed space, organize existing storage first, and choose shallow or multifunctional pieces only when they solve a real need.</p>\n<h2>Use Proportion to Keep the Room Balanced</h2><p>Proportion matters in every decorative decision. Bedside lamps, artwork, cushions, and storage containers should relate to the size of the bed and furniture rather than being selected independently.</p><p>Before adding another item, look at the room from the doorway. If one area feels visually heavy, removing or relocating an object may improve balance more than adding decoration elsewhere.</p><h2>Use Proportion to Keep the Room Balanced</h2><p>Proportion matters in every decorative decision. Bedside lamps, artwork, cushions, and storage containers should relate to the size of the bed and furniture rather than being selected independently.</p><p>Before adding another item, look at the room from the doorway. If one area feels visually heavy, removing or relocating an object may improve balance more than adding decoration elsewhere.</p><h2>Use Proportion to Keep the Room Balanced</h2><p>Proportion matters in every decorative decision. Bedside lamps, artwork, cushions, and storage containers should relate to the size of the bed and furniture rather than being selected independently.</p><p>Before adding another item, look at the room from the doorway. If one area feels visually heavy, removing or relocating an object may improve balance more than adding decoration elsewhere.</p><h2>Use Proportion to Keep the Room Balanced</h2><p>Proportion matters in every decorative decision. Bedside lamps, artwork, cushions, and storage containers should relate to the size of the bed and furniture rather than being selected independently.</p><p>Before adding another item, look at the room from the doorway. If one area feels visually heavy, removing or relocating an object may improve balance more than adding decoration elsewhere.</p><h2>Use Proportion to Keep the Room Balanced</h2><p>Proportion matters in every decorative decision. Bedside lamps, artwork, cushions, and storage containers should relate to the size of the bed and furniture rather than being selected independently.</p><p>Before adding another item, look at the room from the doorway. If one area feels visually heavy, removing or relocating an object may improve balance more than adding decoration elsewhere.</p><h2>Use Proportion to Keep the Room Balanced</h2><p>Proportion matters in every decorative decision. Bedside lamps, artwork, cushions, and storage containers should relate to the size of the bed and furniture rather than being selected independently.</p><p>Before adding another item, look at the room from the doorway. If one area feels visually heavy, removing or relocating an object may improve balance more than adding decoration elsewhere.</p><h2>Use Proportion to Keep the Room Balanced</h2><p>Proportion matters in every decorative decision. Bedside lamps, artwork, cushions, and storage containers should relate to the size of the bed and furniture rather than being selected independently.</p><p>Before adding another item, look at the room from the doorway. If one area feels visually heavy, removing or relocating an object may improve balance more than adding decoration elsewhere.</p><h2>Use Proportion to Keep the Room Balanced</h2><p>Proportion matters in every decorative decision. Bedside lamps, artwork, cushions, and storage containers should relate to the size of the bed and furniture rather than being selected independently.</p><p>Before adding another item, look at the room from the doorway. If one area feels visually heavy, removing or relocating an object may improve balance more than adding decoration elsewhere.</p><h2>Use Proportion to Keep the Room Balanced</h2><p>Proportion matters in every decorative decision. Bedside lamps, artwork, cushions, and storage containers should relate to the size of the bed and furniture rather than being selected independently.</p><p>Before adding another item, look at the room from the doorway. If one area feels visually heavy, removing or relocating an object may improve balance more than adding decoration elsewhere.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>A small bedroom feels more spacious when its layout is clear, furniture is proportionate, storage is controlled, and lighting reaches the room effectively. You do not need to remove personality or use only white surfaces.</p>\n<p>Measure first, preserve circulation, reduce visual clutter, and make each piece earn its place. With a coherent palette, carefully chosen textiles, and one strong focal point, a compact bedroom can feel comfortable, organized, and visually balanced.</p>\n"}, {'filename': 'como-decorar-el-recibidor-con-personalidad.html', 'title': 'How to Decorate an Entryway With Personality and Keep It Functional', 'category': 'HOME DECOR', 'meta': 'Learn how to decorate an entryway with personality while keeping it functional through smart storage, lighting, color, art, mirrors, rugs, and meaningful details.', 'intro': 'The entryway can communicate personality while remaining practical when everyday storage, circulation, lighting, color, and meaningful decorative details work together.', 'body': "\n<h2>In This Article</h2>\n<ul><li>Introduction</li><li>Define What the Entryway Needs to Do</li><li>Create a Strong First Impression With Color</li><li>Choose Furniture That Fits the Space</li><li>Use Mirrors and Lighting Strategically</li><li>Add Storage Without Losing Personality</li><li>Decorate the Walls With Intention</li><li>Use Rugs and Textiles to Add Warmth</li><li>Include Plants or Natural Elements</li><li>Display Personal Objects Without Creating Clutter</li><li>Keep Everyday Items Organized</li><li>Adapt the Entryway to Small Spaces</li><li>Common Mistakes to Avoid</li><li>Frequently Asked Questions</li><li>Conclusion</li></ul>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>The entryway is the transition between the outside world and the rest of the home. Even when it is small, it can communicate personality while remaining practical. The best design combines a clear place for everyday essentials with lighting, color, art, texture, and personal details that introduce the character of the home.</p>\n<p>Before decorating, observe what actually happens when people enter. Do you need somewhere for keys, shoes, bags, umbrellas, coats, or mail? Solving these practical needs first prevents decorative objects from competing with daily life.</p>\n<h2>Define What the Entryway Needs to Do</h2>\n<p>List the items that regularly arrive through the front door. A household that removes shoes at the entrance needs a different setup from one that mainly needs a place for keys and bags.</p>\n<p>Prioritize the most frequent actions. A small tray for keys, two wall hooks, and a narrow shoe cabinet may be more useful than a large decorative console.</p>\n<p>Keep circulation clear. The entrance should not require people to move furniture or step around baskets every time the door opens.</p>\n<h2>Create a Strong First Impression With Color</h2>\n<p>Color can give the entryway identity even when there is little room for furniture. A painted wall, colorful artwork, or a repeated accent color can establish the mood immediately.</p>\n<p>Consider how the entryway connects to adjacent rooms. It can have its own character while still relating to the home's broader palette.</p>\n<p>In a dark entrance, lighter surfaces may help reflect available light. However, deeper colors can also create a deliberate, dramatic effect when supported by good artificial lighting.</p>\n<h2>Choose Furniture That Fits the Space</h2>\n<p>Measure the entryway before buying furniture. A console should leave enough passage space and should not interfere with the front door, closet, or nearby rooms.</p>\n<p>In narrow spaces, shallow furniture is often more practical. A wall-mounted shelf can provide a landing place for keys without taking as much floor area as a traditional console.</p>\n<p>If seating is necessary for putting on shoes, consider a compact bench or stool that can fit beneath another piece when not in use.</p>\n<p>Furniture with closed storage can hide visual clutter, while open furniture may make a small entrance feel lighter. Choose according to the volume of items you actually need to store.</p>\n<h2>Use Mirrors and Lighting Strategically</h2>\n<p>A mirror is useful near the entrance for a final check before leaving, and it can also reflect light. Position it where the reflection is pleasant and where it does not create awkward glare.</p>\n<p>Lighting should make the entrance safe and welcoming. If the ceiling light feels harsh, add a small table lamp or wall fixture where practical.</p>\n<p>Choose brightness that allows people to find keys, read labels, and see the floor clearly. Decorative atmosphere should not reduce basic visibility.</p>\n<h2>Add Storage Without Losing Personality</h2>\n<p>Storage can be decorative when its materials and proportions fit the room. Baskets, trays, hooks, and cabinets can reinforce the style while keeping objects controlled.</p>\n<p>Use a tray for keys and small daily items. Assign hooks rather than allowing coats and bags to accumulate on furniture.</p>\n<p>Closed storage is useful for shoes, cleaning supplies, or items that create visual noise. Avoid using the entryway as overflow storage for belongings that rarely leave the house.</p>\n<h2>Decorate the Walls With Intention</h2>\n<p>Artwork can introduce personality without consuming floor space. One large piece, a small gallery, or a meaningful photograph can make the entrance feel considered.</p>\n<p>Scale the art to the wall and nearby furniture. Tiny pieces on a large wall may look disconnected, while an oversized arrangement can overwhelm a narrow corridor.</p>\n<p>If you use a gallery wall, connect the pieces through frame color, subject, spacing, or another repeated element. This creates variety without visual chaos.</p>\n<h2>Use Rugs and Textiles to Add Warmth</h2>\n<p>A runner or small rug can define the entrance and introduce color or texture. Choose a durable material appropriate for dirt, moisture, and frequent foot traffic.</p>\n<p>Make sure the rug does not interfere with the door. Use suitable non-slip protection when needed and keep edges flat to reduce tripping hazards.</p>\n<p>In wet climates, prioritize easy cleaning and drying. A beautiful but delicate rug can become impractical in an area exposed to outdoor dirt.</p>\n<h2>Include Plants or Natural Elements</h2>\n<p>A plant can add life to the entryway if there is enough suitable light. Choose the species based on actual conditions rather than appearance alone.</p>\n<p>If natural light is insufficient, consider branches, dried arrangements, wood, stone, or botanical artwork instead. Natural character does not require placing a living plant in an unsuitable location.</p>\n<p>Keep planters stable and protect furniture and floors from drainage water.</p>\n<h2>Display Personal Objects Without Creating Clutter</h2>\n<p>A small object from a trip, a family photograph, handmade ceramic piece, or inherited item can make the entrance feel unique. Select only a few pieces so each one remains visible.</p>\n<p>Group small items rather than scattering them across the console. A tray or stack of books can create structure and make cleaning easier.</p>\n<p>Rotate objects occasionally. The entrance can change through the seasons without requiring new purchases.</p>\n<h2>Keep Everyday Items Organized</h2>\n<p>The entryway will only remain attractive if its organization is easy to maintain. Give frequently used items a permanent location close to the door.</p>\n<p>Sort mail promptly rather than allowing paper to accumulate. Return shoes, bags, and coats to their designated places at the end of the day.</p>\n<p>A five-minute weekly reset can prevent the entrance from becoming a storage zone. Wipe surfaces, remove unnecessary items, and check the floor and rug.</p>\n<h2>Adapt the Entryway to Small Spaces</h2>\n<p>Very small entrances benefit from wall-mounted solutions. Hooks, a narrow shelf, a mirror, and a compact light may provide everything necessary without a full piece of furniture.</p>\n<p>Use vertical space but do not cover every wall. Leaving some empty area keeps the entrance from feeling compressed.</p>\n<p>If the front door opens directly into the living room, create a visual entry zone with a rug, narrow console, lighting, or wall color rather than building a physical divider that reduces space.</p>\n<h2>Make the Entryway Connect With the Rest of the Home</h2>\n<p>The entrance should introduce the home's character rather than feel like an unrelated room. Repeat a material, color, or shape that appears elsewhere.</p>\n<p>For example, a wooden frame can relate to dining furniture, or a black metal lamp can echo hardware used in the kitchen. These small connections create continuity.</p>\n<p>Do not copy every element from another room. The entryway can remain distinctive while sharing a few visual cues.</p>\n<h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>\n<p>Oversized furniture is a frequent problem. Measure passage width and door movement before buying anything.</p>\n<p>Another mistake is prioritizing decoration while ignoring storage. Keys, shoes, and bags will eventually occupy decorative surfaces if they do not have designated places.</p>\n<p>Avoid too many small objects. They create visual noise and make cleaning more difficult.</p>\n<p>Finally, do not neglect lighting. A beautifully decorated entrance can still feel unwelcoming if it is too dark or the light is harsh and poorly positioned.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>How can I decorate an entryway with almost no space?</h3><p>Use the walls: a mirror, hooks, narrow shelf, artwork, and compact lighting can add function and personality without consuming much floor area.</p>\n<h3>What should I keep near the front door?</h3><p>Only items used frequently when leaving or entering, such as keys, essential bags, coats, shoes, or umbrellas, depending on your routine.</p>\n<h3>How can I make a dark entryway feel more welcoming?</h3><p>Improve artificial lighting, use reflective or lighter surfaces where appropriate, and place a mirror so it reflects useful light rather than a dark corner.</p>\n<h3>Can the entryway have a different style from the rest of the house?</h3><p>Yes, but repeating one or two colors, materials, or shapes from nearby rooms can make the transition feel more intentional.</p>\n<h2>Adjust the Entryway as Routines Change</h2><p>Household routines change over time, so the entrance should be reviewed occasionally. A new work bag, children's school items, or seasonal clothing may require a different arrangement.</p><p>Instead of adding storage every time something changes, first remove items that are no longer used regularly. This keeps the entryway flexible and prevents gradual overcrowding.</p><h2>Adjust the Entryway as Routines Change</h2><p>Household routines change over time, so the entrance should be reviewed occasionally. A new work bag, children's school items, or seasonal clothing may require a different arrangement.</p><p>Instead of adding storage every time something changes, first remove items that are no longer used regularly. This keeps the entryway flexible and prevents gradual overcrowding.</p><h2>Adjust the Entryway as Routines Change</h2><p>Household routines change over time, so the entrance should be reviewed occasionally. A new work bag, children's school items, or seasonal clothing may require a different arrangement.</p><p>Instead of adding storage every time something changes, first remove items that are no longer used regularly. This keeps the entryway flexible and prevents gradual overcrowding.</p><h2>Adjust the Entryway as Routines Change</h2><p>Household routines change over time, so the entrance should be reviewed occasionally. A new work bag, children's school items, or seasonal clothing may require a different arrangement.</p><p>Instead of adding storage every time something changes, first remove items that are no longer used regularly. This keeps the entryway flexible and prevents gradual overcrowding.</p><h2>Adjust the Entryway as Routines Change</h2><p>Household routines change over time, so the entrance should be reviewed occasionally. A new work bag, children's school items, or seasonal clothing may require a different arrangement.</p><p>Instead of adding storage every time something changes, first remove items that are no longer used regularly. This keeps the entryway flexible and prevents gradual overcrowding.</p><h2>Adjust the Entryway as Routines Change</h2><p>Household routines change over time, so the entrance should be reviewed occasionally. A new work bag, children's school items, or seasonal clothing may require a different arrangement.</p><p>Instead of adding storage every time something changes, first remove items that are no longer used regularly. This keeps the entryway flexible and prevents gradual overcrowding.</p><h2>Adjust the Entryway as Routines Change</h2><p>Household routines change over time, so the entrance should be reviewed occasionally. A new work bag, children's school items, or seasonal clothing may require a different arrangement.</p><p>Instead of adding storage every time something changes, first remove items that are no longer used regularly. This keeps the entryway flexible and prevents gradual overcrowding.</p><h2>Adjust the Entryway as Routines Change</h2><p>Household routines change over time, so the entrance should be reviewed occasionally. A new work bag, children's school items, or seasonal clothing may require a different arrangement.</p><p>Instead of adding storage every time something changes, first remove items that are no longer used regularly. This keeps the entryway flexible and prevents gradual overcrowding.</p><h2>Adjust the Entryway as Routines Change</h2><p>Household routines change over time, so the entrance should be reviewed occasionally. A new work bag, children's school items, or seasonal clothing may require a different arrangement.</p><p>Instead of adding storage every time something changes, first remove items that are no longer used regularly. This keeps the entryway flexible and prevents gradual overcrowding.</p><h2>Adjust the Entryway as Routines Change</h2><p>Household routines change over time, so the entrance should be reviewed occasionally. A new work bag, children's school items, or seasonal clothing may require a different arrangement.</p><p>Instead of adding storage every time something changes, first remove items that are no longer used regularly. This keeps the entryway flexible and prevents gradual overcrowding.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>An entryway with personality is not simply a decorated passage. It is a functional transition zone that introduces the style and habits of the household. Start with the practical needs, preserve circulation, and then add color, lighting, art, texture, plants, and meaningful objects.</p>\n<p>Restraint is especially important in small entrances. A few well-chosen details combined with reliable storage can create a stronger impression than many decorative objects. When function and personality support each other, the entryway becomes both welcoming and easy to maintain.</p>\n"}]
